It will be seen from (3), that the value of <math>\text{Q}</math>, initially zero, increases to a maximum and then decays; finally, according to an exponential law, with the period of the more slowly transformed product, whether <math>\text{A}</math> or <math>\text{B}</math>.

(''b'') A primary source supplies the matter <math>\text{A}</math> at a constant rate, and the process has continued so long that the amounts of the products <math>\text{A}</math>, <math>\text{B}</math>, <math>\text{C}</math> have reached a steady limiting value. The primary source is then suddenly removed. It is required to find the amounts of <math>\text{A}</math>, <math>\text{B}</math> and <math>\text{C}</math> remaining at any subsequent time <math>t</math>.

In this case of equilibrium, the number <math>n_0</math> of particles of <math>\text{A}</math> supplied per second from the source is equal to the number of particles which change into <math>\text{B}</math> per second, and also of <math>\text{B}</math> into <math>\text{C}</math>. This requires the relation
{{block center|<math>n_0 = \lambda_1\text{P}_0 = y_2\text{Q}_0 = \lambda_3\text{R}_0</math>}}
where <math>\text{P}_0</math>, <math>\text{Q}_0</math>, <math>\text{R}_0</math> are the initial number of particles of <math>\text{A}</math>, <math>\text{B}</math>, <math>\text{C}</math> present, and <math>\lambda_1</math>, <math>\lambda_2</math>, <math>\lambda_3</math> are their constants of transformation.

